    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-8">UK Electronic Communications Act 2000</h1>

    <h2>The Legal Landscape in the UK</h2>
    <p>In the UK, electronic signatures are governed primarily by the Electronic Communications Act 2000 and the UK eIDAS Regulations (retained EU law post-Brexit).</p>

    <p>Under English law, a contract is generally valid if there is an offer, acceptance, consideration, and an intention to create legal relations. There is rarely a requirement for a contract to be in a specific format, meaning electronic signatures are widely accepted.</p>

    <h2>When is a wet signature still required?</h2>
    <p>Certain documents still require physical signatures or specific witnessing requirements (deeds, some property transactions, wills, and certain HR documents like settlement agreements). For standard B2B contracts, NDAs, and invoices, electronic signatures via platforms like usignpdf are perfectly valid.</p>

    <h2>The Law Commission View (2019)</h2>
    <p>In September 2019, the UK Law Commission published a comprehensive report confirming that electronic signatures are valid for executing documents, including deeds, provided there is an intention to authenticate and any required formalities are met.</p>
    <p>The Commission explicitly noted that an electronic signature can take many forms, including:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Typing a name into a contract or email.</li>
        <li>Pasting a digitized image of a handwritten signature.</li>
        <li>Clicking an "I accept" button on a website.</li>
        <li>Using a touchscreen to write a name.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The core requirement remains the <em>intent to authenticate</em>, which is why utilizing a platform with a robust <a href="/security/audit-trails">Audit Trail</a> is highly recommended over simple email exchanges.</p>

